<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#luxembourgish">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourgish</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxembourgish is a West Germanic language, derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> Franconian dialects, spoken as a mother tongue by most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgers">Luxembourgers</a>. Long regarded as a German dialect, it became a symbol of national identity during and after the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and a law of 1984 declared it the national language. French and German remain languages of administration, law and the press alongside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxembourgish.jpg" alt="Luxembourgish" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Erren · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moselfrankisch.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Luxembourgish belongs to the West Central German group of the High German languages and is the primary example of a Moselle Franconian language. It is closely related to Transylvanian Saxon, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a> have spoken in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> since the High Middle Ages. The National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> has described the Codex Mariendalensis, a poem of nearly 6,000 lines about the life of the 13th-century Yolanda of Vianden, as the earliest known text in Luxembourgish. It was discovered in 1999 at Ansembourg Castle. The language was considered a German dialect until about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and then created its own standard form in vocabulary, grammar and spelling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Luxembourgish has three genders and three cases, nominative, accusative and dative. The genitive survives only in a few phrases, and its work is normally done by the dative with a possessive, as in dem Mann säi Buch, literally the man his book. Like German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> it is a verb-second language. Women and girls are most often referred to with the neuter pronoun hatt. It has borrowed many French words, and its newest vocabulary comes from English. Several dialects exist, grouped into North, East, South and Center forms, and mobility and mass media are leading gradually towards a standard Luxembourgish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Luxembourgish is the national language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and one of its three administrative languages, with German and French. About 400,000 people speak it worldwide. It is also spoken in the Arelerland region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, where French has since nearly replaced it, and in small parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Proposals for a standard spelling go back to the middle of the 19th century. The first official system, adopted on 5 June 1946, won little approval. The standard that became official on 10 October 1975 grew out of the Luxemburger Wörterbuch, published in five volumes from 1950 to 1977, and was modified in a reform of 30 July 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luxembourgish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Society remained overwhelmingly Catholic, and the spoken language, <b>Luxembourgish</b>, coexisted with French and German, which served as the languages of law, administration and the press; a law of 1984 declared Luxembourgish the national language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
